WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:06.546
LiveWorld turns a tabletop into a game-system
experiment. This demo uses clearly labelled,
AI-generated

00:06.546 --> 00:13.092
images: a cup is a water source, blocks are homes,
and two wooden sticks

00:13.092 --> 00:14.027
become bridges.

00:15.000 --> 00:21.950
Muse Spark locates the objects and suggests their
roles. SAM traces their shapes. These

00:21.950 --> 00:28.901
are actual returned masks, not hand-drawn overlays.
The creator reviews every role before any

00:28.901 --> 00:30.887
object enters the simulation.

00:35.000 --> 00:41.300
After confirmation, image-relative positions map to
a fixed grid. Couriers move, water is produced,

00:41.300 --> 00:47.600
and each household has a deadline. The simulator
computes every delivery and queue; the

00:47.600 --> 00:50.300
model does not invent the outcome.

00:51.000 --> 00:57.024
Here is a replay of two actual Meta captures. Only
the lower bridge changes

00:57.024 --> 01:03.048
grid position. Waiting drops from twenty-two to ten
turns, but on-time delivery remains twenty

01:03.048 --> 01:08.211
out of twenty-four. A useful change is not
automatically a solved level.

01:10.000 --> 01:17.150
Now switch to an authored level: six couriers,
twenty-four units, fixed household deadlines. Twenty

01:17.150 --> 01:24.300
arrive on time. This recording replays a real Meta
playtest, with every result recalculated.

01:25.000 --> 01:31.256
The agent's balanced-dispatch proposal looks
plausible, but only fourteen units arrive on time.
Code

01:31.256 --> 01:37.513
marks the trial as unsuccessful. A model's confident
explanation cannot turn a failed experiment

01:37.513 --> 01:39.300
into a successful one.

01:40.000 --> 01:46.387
Moving the south bridge is a verified fix: all
twenty-four units arrive on time,

01:46.387 --> 01:52.775
with the original six couriers, demand and
deadlines. Waiting increases from sixteen to
twenty-two

01:52.775 --> 01:58.706
turns, so that trade-off stays visible. Success is
checked independently by the simulator.

02:00.000 --> 02:05.661
Creators can also change the game itself. Here the
instruction asks Muse Spark to

02:05.661 --> 02:09.300
use eight couriers while keeping every other rule
unchanged.

02:10.000 --> 02:16.590
The instruction becomes a validated proposal. Only
couriers change from six to eight. This

02:16.590 --> 02:21.767
creator design change is clearly separated from a
same-resource playtest fix.

02:23.000 --> 02:28.996
Apply the candidate to keep designing. LiveWorld
saves the original as a checkpoint. You

02:28.996 --> 02:34.992
can compare against it later, change your mind, or
branch from a different layout.

02:38.000 --> 02:44.328
Save the whole project, including photos,
checkpoints and trials. Reopening it validates the
inputs

02:44.328 --> 02:49.300
and recalculates the measurements, instead of
trusting a saved success flag.

02:50.000 --> 02:58.680
Export inspectable evidence. LiveWorld helps game
creators test fixed-camera tabletop layouts through
deterministic, replayable

02:58.680 --> 02:59.300
experiments.
